// `protected init` is `private init` plus subclasses — and this file is neither.
// A module that merely imports Dial is not a subclass of it, so it may not
// construct one directly; it must go through the module's own factory.
//
// This is the case that pins `protected` as a genuine restriction on
// constructors rather than a decorative synonym for `public`. Its counterpart —
// the same-module factory, which `protected` must keep permitting to stay
// monotone with `private` — is in test/pass/protected_from_subclass.sf.
//
// The third case, a SUBCLASS in another module constructing the base, is accepted
// by the checker but cannot be a runnable pass test: subclassing a named-imported
// class emits an undefined LLVM symbol for the inherited method, a pre-existing
// codegen bug independent of any modifier (it reproduces with a plain public
// init). See docs/design/access-modifiers.md §5b.
import "../support/visibility_protected_ctor.sf" as DialMod

var d: DialMod.Dial = DialMod.Dial(42)
IO.println(d.get())
